KG42.register("nodes-d05e6df6652c97cec03fe20a",{"id":"COMP-535650ecb2e046e9","code":"PRC002","label":"Per-contract two-sided ascending pricing with contract-selection feedback","definition":"A bilateral contract price formation process in which per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices serve as negotiation states, are initialized from zero, iteratively increase according to contract-selection feedback, and complete when no price changes occur in an entire round.","layer":"L6","template_code":"BCT","concept_id":"coordination.price_signal","concept_ids":["coordination.price_signal","pricing.local"],"identity_dimensions":{"definition":"A bilateral contract price formation process in which per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices serve as negotiation states, are initialized from zero, iteratively increase according to contract-selection feedback, and complete when no price changes occur in an entire round.","object":"Per-transaction bilateral contract prices with explicit buyers and sellers and their negotiation states; the subject matter of energy transactions and demand-reduction flexibility transactions is retained separately.","inputs":"Potential bilateral transactions, transaction parties, selectable contracts, contract-selection feedback, and current buy-side and sell-side prices; the feasible contract domain and price-adjustment parameters are specified according to the source.","operation":"Initialize the per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices to zero, increase the relevant prices according to member rules based on contract-selection feedback, and repeat the negotiation until, in an entire round, both-side prices for all transactions show no change.","outputs":"Negotiation price states for per-transaction trades and the agreed prices of concluded contracts; the distinct variable roles of updated price pairs and final contract prices are retained.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT price formation. Contract selection serves as price feedback; contract configuration, payment execution, and resource scheduling are not included in the parent item; buyer price, seller price, and contract price are not presumed equal, and the specific increment formulas of the two members are not unified."},"exclusions":[["Applies to forward and real-time energy trading markets m∈{F,R}, processing per transaction ω∈Ω_m.","The trigger condition is retained as ω∈Y*_{m b(ω)} and ω∈Z_{m s(ω)}\\Z*_{m s(ω)}; prices are adjusted only when the buyer selects and the seller rejects.","If p^b_ω>p^s_ω, then p^s_ω←p^s_ω+Δp; otherwise p^b_ω←p^b_ω+Δp. Δp is the minimum price difference that can be specified by the contract.","The two-sided prices are initialized from zero; broader numerical domains and units are not given and are not added.","The direct target is retained as price-pair updating; the source describes the post-termination equilibrium arrangement using the buyer price vector, and the contract-network stability guarantee retains its perfect-substitutability condition."],["The subject matter is distribution-network demand-reduction flexibility; each contract specifies the same reduction energy and is discretized by energy and time, and each unit can be priced separately.","The contract retains the (ω,t_ω,p_ω) structure and the specific retail metering period; limited energy-storage capacity may make contract preferences in different time periods interdependent.","In each round, select the preferred upstream and downstream contract bundles and raise the prices of contracts with excess demand; the available citation does not provide the two comparison branches of 03 or the same Δp, so these details are not transplanted.","Termination requires that, for all ω∈Ω, the two-sided prices respectively satisfy p^{b,(k+1)}_ω=p^{b,(k)}_ω and p^{s,(k+1)}_ω=p^{s,(k)}_ω.","In the feasible outcome X′⊆X, each ω∈τ(X′) corresponds to at most one contract, so a selected transaction has a unique contract price; this is not extended to all potential transactions being concluded.","p_ω denotes the amount paid by the buyer to the seller for the contracted demand reduction; its contract-price role is retained and is not directly equated with the two-sided prices in the negotiation."]],"members":[{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Agents specify potential upstream trades with sellers before initializing the buyer and seller prices used in price-signal coordination. Scope: Initialization of each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its seller price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price exceeds the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price exceeds the seller price. Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its buyer price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price does not exceed the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price does not exceed the seller price. The price-adjustment increment is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. Scope: Price-adjustment process for forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. Each trade's buyer and seller prices are initialized to zero. Scope: Each trade in the forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. Condition: At initialization of the price-adjustment process. The price-adjustment process is complete when an iteration produces no price changes. Scope: Price-adjustment process for each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. Condition: No price changes occur during an iteration.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Each trade in the forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. | Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. | Initialization of each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. | Price-adjustment process for each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. | Price-adjustment process for forward and real-time bilateral contract markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"4bd36c24518f6f5fab710ed900c291f2520134c5a3db6ac1c1ce32d3347bc113","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"The distributed price-adjustment process coordinates buyers' offers and sellers' acceptance or rejection to find an agreed bilateral contract network that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from. Scope: Forward and real-time bilateral P2P markets; agents selecting contracts with potential counterparties. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability. The contract-network topology represents physical and informational restrictions on which agents can trade with one another. Scope: Potential counterparty relationships in the forward and real-time bilateral contract networks. Each agent starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Scope: Initialization of the price-adjustment process in each forward and real-time market; all agents. Each agent selects upstream and downstream contract bundles according to its own utility-maximizing choice over available contract subsets. Scope: Individual contract selection during each iteration of the forward and real-time price-adjustment process. The two generators can trade directly with one another, and the supplier intermediates between the generators and the prosumers. Scope: Contract-network topology used in case studies A and B. The methodology does not preclude introducing direct trading relationships between generators and prosumers. Scope: Permitted extensions of the proposed bilateral P2P methodology beyond the illustrated case-study topology.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Contract-network topology used in case studies A and B. | Forward and real-time bilateral P2P markets; agents selecting contracts with potential counterparties. | Individual contract selection during each iteration of the forward and real-time price-adjustment process. | Initialization of the price-adjustment process in each forward and real-time market; all agents. | Permitted extensions of the proposed bilateral P2P methodology beyond the illustrated case-study topology. | Potential counterparty relationships in the forward and real-time bilateral contract networks.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E26"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"57b80f5357472bfac8b122e16f147b8129b75e8c1cd7e81fa6a785adc9dca8cc","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004","node_id":"coordination.price_signal","function":"coordination.price_signal","unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Iterative price-signal coordination produces mutually agreed prices for local flexibility contracts. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. Condition: At negotiation completion, when no price changes occur during an iteration. Each discrete unit of flexibility may be individually priced. Scope: Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time. Each trade is associated with at most one contract and therefore has a unique price. Scope: Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market. Condition: The selected contract set is a feasible market outcome.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. | Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time. | Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e24"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"88523e59a0f17544ed3a8e30da1ef9de220d511251634127669b5e931c1b792b","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"The agreed local contract price specifies the amount the seller receives from the buyer for the contracted demand reduction. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. Condition: For contracts agreed by their buyer and seller. Payments made by the DSO match the combined revenue accumulated by prosumers and aggregators in the reported outcomes. Scope: Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market. Prosumers 46-55 receive no flexibility-market revenue because they do not participate. Scope: Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. Condition: These agents do not participate in the flexibility market. Prosumers 1-30 must be compensated for their higher battery degradation costs and consequently receive significantly more revenue in Case Study B. Scope: Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. Condition: Their battery degradation costs are higher in Case Study B.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. | Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. | Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. | Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"c24209a69140811d9a59109ee0ae831784f62369582751c594a22ad2a16294e4","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007","node_id":"pricing.local","function":"pricing.local","unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":false}],"paper_ids":["03-bilateral_p2p","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"p in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually dev","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\Delta p be the increment of the price-adjustment process, which is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. For market m \\in \\{F, R\\}: 1) Each agent i \\in \\mathcal{A} starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Let the full set of trades in the market be given by \\Omega_m. For agent i, the set of potential upstream trades is given by \\Omega^B_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = b(\\omega)\\} and the set of potential downstream trades is given by \\Omega^S_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = s(\\omega)\\}. 2) Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m has a buyer price p^b_\\omega and a seller price p^s_\\omega. Initially, p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Let \\Delta p be the increment of the price-adjustment process, which is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. For market m \\in \\{F, R\\}: 1) Each agent i \\in \\mathcal{A} starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Let the full set of trades in the market be given by \\Omega_m. For agent i, the set of potential upstream trades is given by \\Omega^B_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = b(\\omega)\\} and the set of potential downstream trades is given by \\Omega^S_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = s(\\omega)\\}. 2) Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m has a buyer price p^b_\\omega and a seller price p^s_\\omega. Initially, p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Contract Networks for Energy Trading","anchor_text":"p y p II (ω, pω), where ω ∈ m is the underlying trade and pω is hin the price speciﬁed by the contra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each trade ω has a buyer b(ω) ∈ A and a seller s(ω) ∈ A. These are also the buyer and seller for each contract involving ω, i.e., for contract x = (ω, p_ω) we denote its buyer b(x) := b(ω) and seller s(x) := s(ω). The agents involved with a set of contracts X'_m ⊆ X_m are given by a(X'_m) := {∪_{x∈X'_m}b(x), s(x)}. Agent i has upstream contracts Y_mi := {y ∈ X_m|i = b(y)}, and downstream contracts Z_mi := {z ∈ X_m|i = s(x)}.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"Each trade ω has a buyer b(ω) ∈ A and a seller s(ω) ∈ A. These are also the buyer and seller for each contract involving ω, i.e., for contract x = (ω, p_ω) we denote its buyer b(x) := b(ω) and seller s(x) := s(ω). The agents involved with a set of contracts X'_m ⊆ X_m are given by a(X'_m) := {∪_{x∈X'_m}b(x), s(x)}. Agent i has upstream contracts Y_mi := {y ∈ X_m|i = b(y)}, and downstream contracts Z_mi := {z ∈ X_m|i = s(x)}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"3) Iteratively: he a) Each agent i ∈A constructs a set of upstream p- contracts to choose from: Ymi ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"d) For each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m, the buyer and seller prices are adjusted as follows: if \\omega \\in Y^*_{mb(\\omega)} and \\omega \\in Z_{ms(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{ms(\\omega)} then if p^b_\\omega > p^s_\\omega then p^s_\\omega \\leftarrow p^s_\\omega + \\Delta p else p^b_\\omega \\leftarrow p^b_\\omega + \\Delta p end if end if","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"d) For each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m, the buyer and seller prices are adjusted as follows: if \\omega \\in Y^*_{mb(\\omega)} and \\omega \\in Z_{ms(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{ms(\\omega)} then if p^b_\\omega > p^s_\\omega then p^s_\\omega \\leftarrow p^s_\\omega + \\Delta p else p^b_\\omega \\leftarrow p^b_\\omega + \\Delta p end if end if"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"am li- e) The price-adjustment process is complete when no est price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"e) The price-adjustment process is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. 4) The arrangement [\\tau(\\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}) | p^b] is a competitive equilibrium, where p^b is the vector of buyer prices for the trades in \\Omega_m. This coincides with a stable outcome, \\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","quote":"e) The price-adjustment process is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. 4) The arrangement [\\tau(\\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}) | p^b] is a competitive equilibrium, where p^b is the vector of buyer prices for the trades in \\Omega_m. This coincides with a stable outcome, \\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"ENERGY TRADING 2031 al- The process is scalable, since, at each iteration, only the lowest price rem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The process is scalable, since, at each iteration, only the lowest price remaining upstream contract and highest price remaining downstream need to be considered, and the number of available contracts is reduced. ... The price-adjustment process is completed for the forward and real-time markets, so that in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually deviate from. The price-adjustment process is constructed with buyers making progressively higher offers for trades to potential sellers, and sellers choosing to accept or reject the offers they receive.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"The process is scalable, since, at each iteration, only the lowest price remaining upstream contract and highest price remaining downstream need to be considered, and the number of available contracts is reduced. ... The price-adjustment process is completed for the forward and real-time markets, so that in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually deviate from. The price-adjustment process is constructed with buyers making progressively higher offers for trades to potential sellers, and sellers choosing to accept or reject the offers they receive."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":8,"printed_page":"2033","section":"VI. Conclusion","anchor_text":"ers market uncertainty. Utility-maximising preferences for real- are time contracts and forward cont","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"A new scalable market design for P2P energy trading has been presented, using bilateral contract networks. The design includes forward and real-time markets and incorporates important characteristics particular to energy trading, including upstream-downstream energy balance and forward market uncertainty. Utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts have been presented for different types of electricity market participants. It has been shown that these preferences satisfy full substitutability conditions, which ensures a distributed price-adjustment process can be used to find an agreed network of contracts that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from.","paper":"03-bilateral_p2p","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","quote":"A new scalable market design for P2P energy trading has been presented, using bilateral contract networks. The design includes forward and real-time markets and incorporates important characteristics particular to energy trading, including upstream-downstream energy balance and forward market uncertainty. Utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts have been presented for different types of electricity market participants. It has been shown that these preferences satisfy full substitutability conditions, which ensures a distributed price-adjustment process can be used to find an agreed network of contracts that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e24","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2133 The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. \\cup_{i\\in A} Y^{*}_i is a stable outcome for the trading network.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. \\cup_{i\\in A} Y^{*}_i is a stable outcome for the trading network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ω ∈Ω. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with dow their neighbors. Each iteration: no r","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A flow chart of the price-negotiation process is shown in Fig. 2. Each agent i \\in A starts by specifying the flexibility trades they may be willing to make. Let \\Omega be the full set of trades in the market. Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega is assigned a buyer price p^{b}_{\\omega} and a seller price p^{s}_{\\omega}. Initially, p^{b}_{\\omega} = 0 and p^{s}_{\\omega} = 0 for all \\omega \\in \\Omega. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with their neighbors. Each iteration: Each agent i \\in A constructs a set of upstream contracts to choose from, Y_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{b}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, b(\\omega) = i\\} and a set of downstream contracts to choose from, Z_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{s}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, s(\\omega) = i\\}.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"A flow chart of the price-negotiation process is shown in Fig. 2. Each agent i \\in A starts by specifying the flexibility trades they may be willing to make. Let \\Omega be the full set of trades in the market. Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega is assigned a buyer price p^{b}_{\\omega} and a seller price p^{s}_{\\omega}. Initially, p^{b}_{\\omega} = 0 and p^{s}_{\\omega} = 0 for all \\omega \\in \\Omega. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with their neighbors. Each iteration: Each agent i \\in A constructs a set of upstream contracts to choose from, Y_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{b}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, b(\\omega) = i\\} and a set of downstream contracts to choose from, Z_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{s}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, s(\\omega) = i\\}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Each contract specifies the same energy quantity of demand reduction, but an agent may accept multiple contracts for each time interval. The contracts discretize the prosumers’ energy flexibility (in terms of energy and time), and allow each discrete unit to be individually priced. Multi-interval trading is important, since prosumers may have storage systems with finite energy capacity. In this case, their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of demand reduction during a particular interval will depend on the price and availability of contracts at other time intervals.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"Each contract specifies the same energy quantity of demand reduction, but an agent may accept multiple contracts for each time interval. The contracts discretize the prosumers’ energy flexibility (in terms of energy and time), and allow each discrete unit to be individually priced. Multi-interval trading is important, since prosumers may have storage systems with finite energy capacity. In this case, their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of demand reduction during a particular interval will depend on the price and availability of contracts at other time intervals."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","quote":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"[44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their th individual net energy use (e.g. dur","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The proposed flexibility market works alongside existing retail electricity market arrangements in a distribution network. It is assumed that the distribution network has a number of prosumers–consumers with flexible energy resources, and equipment for automated resource scheduling based on user settings, such as a building or home energy management system [44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their individual net energy use (e.g. during each 30 minute interval of the day). For each interval, each prosumer is charged for their net energy use, at prices which may be time-of-use dependent, and paid for their net exported energy [45].","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The proposed flexibility market works alongside existing retail electricity market arrangements in a distribution network. It is assumed that the distribution network has a number of prosumers–consumers with flexible energy resources, and equipment for automated resource scheduling based on user settings, such as a building or home energy management system [44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their individual net energy use (e.g. during each 30 minute interval of the day). For each interval, each prosumer is charged for their net energy use, at prices which may be time-of-use dependent, and paid for their net exported energy [45]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design; Fig. 1","anchor_text":"mer xis- ce- ies ar- Fig. 1. A trading network for distribution system ﬂexibility between a DSO, , i","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The flexibility market is made up of a network of potential flexibility contracts, with multiple contracts connecting each pair of agents that are able to trade with one another. Fig. 1 shows a high-level diagram of a trading network for distribution system flexibility. Each flexibility contract specifies a price the seller will receive from the buyer for reducing demand during a particular retail market metering interval.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The flexibility market is made up of a network of potential flexibility contracts, with multiple contracts connecting each pair of agents that are able to trade with one another. Fig. 1 shows a high-level diagram of a trading network for distribution system flexibility. Each flexibility contract specifies a price the seller will receive from the buyer for reducing demand during a particular retail market metering interval."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration.","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"MORSTYN et al.: DESIGNING DECENTRALIZED MARKETS FOR DISTRIBUTION SYSTE large number of consumers and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Let A be the set of agents in the trading network. In the flexibility market, the agents trade flexibility contracts for a set of time intervals \\(\\mathcal{T} = \\{1, \\ldots, T\\}\\). Each flexibility contract specifies a price for a flexibility trade, specifying an agreed reduction in demand during a particular time interval. Note that all trades specify the same energy quantity of demand reduction. Let \\(X\\) be the set of contracts between the agents. A contract \\(x \\in X\\) can be described by a tuple \\((\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\), where \\(\\omega \\in \\Omega\\) is a unique trade ID, \\(t_\\omega\\) is the time interval of the underlying trade and \\(p_\\omega\\) is the price specified by the contract for the trade. The trades underlying a set of contracts \\(X\\) are given by \\(\\tau(X) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega | (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega) \\in X\\}\\).","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","quote":"Let A be the set of agents in the trading network. In the flexibility market, the agents trade flexibility contracts for a set of time intervals \\(\\mathcal{T} = \\{1, \\ldots, T\\}\\). Each flexibility contract specifies a price for a flexibility trade, specifying an agreed reduction in demand during a particular time interval. Note that all trades specify the same energy quantity of demand reduction. Let \\(X\\) be the set of contracts between the agents. A contract \\(x \\in X\\) can be described by a tuple \\((\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\), where \\(\\omega \\in \\Omega\\) is a unique trade ID, \\(t_\\omega\\) is the time interval of the underlying trade and \\(p_\\omega\\) is the price specified by the contract for the trade. The trades underlying a set of contracts \\(X\\) are given by \\(\\tau(X) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega | (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega) \\in X\\}\\)."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["bilateral_conditional_price_update","negotiated_individual_contract_price"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L6-f5ce8673e5","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L6","ontological_kind":"bilateral_conditional_price_update"},"semantic_definition":{"definition":"A bilateral contract price formation process in which per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices serve as negotiation states, are initialized from zero, iteratively increase according to contract-selection feedback, and complete when no price changes occur in an entire round.","object":"Per-transaction bilateral contract prices with explicit buyers and sellers and their negotiation states; the subject matter of energy transactions and demand-reduction flexibility transactions is retained separately.","inputs":"Potential bilateral transactions, transaction parties, selectable contracts, contract-selection feedback, and current buy-side and sell-side prices; the feasible contract domain and price-adjustment parameters are specified according to the source.","operation":"Initialize the per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices to zero, increase the relevant prices according to member rules based on contract-selection feedback, and repeat the negotiation until, in an entire round, both-side prices for all transactions show no change.","outputs":"Negotiation price states for per-transaction trades and the agreed prices of concluded contracts; the distinct variable roles of updated price pairs and final contract prices are retained.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT price formation. Contract selection serves as price feedback; contract configuration, payment execution, and resource scheduling are not included in the parent item; buyer price, seller price, and contract price are not presumed equal, and the specific increment formulas of the two members are not unified."},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","paper_id":"03-bilateral_p2p","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","source_occurrences":[{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R002","market_meaning":"Agents specify potential upstream trades with sellers before initializing the buyer and seller prices used in price-signal coordination. Scope: Initialization of each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its seller price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price exceeds the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price exceeds the seller price. Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its buyer price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price does not exceed the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price does not exceed the seller price. The price-adjustment increment is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. Scope: Price-adjustment process for forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. Each trade's buyer and seller prices are initialized to zero. Scope: Each trade in the forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. Condition: At initialization of the price-adjustment process. The price-adjustment process is complete when an iteration produces no price changes. Scope: Price-adjustment process for each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. Condition: No price changes occur during an iteration.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Each trade in the forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. | Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. | Initialization of each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. | Price-adjustment process for each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. | Price-adjustment process for forward and real-time bilateral contract markets.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E18"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006"],"decision_anchor":"coordination.price_signal","source_rule_sha256":"4bd36c24518f6f5fab710ed900c291f2520134c5a3db6ac1c1ce32d3347bc113"},{"id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::V::coordination.price_signal","rule_id":"v141-c-R004","market_meaning":"The distributed price-adjustment process coordinates buyers' offers and sellers' acceptance or rejection to find an agreed bilateral contract network that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from. Scope: Forward and real-time bilateral P2P markets; agents selecting contracts with potential counterparties. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability. The contract-network topology represents physical and informational restrictions on which agents can trade with one another. Scope: Potential counterparty relationships in the forward and real-time bilateral contract networks. Each agent starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Scope: Initialization of the price-adjustment process in each forward and real-time market; all agents. Each agent selects upstream and downstream contract bundles according to its own utility-maximizing choice over available contract subsets. Scope: Individual contract selection during each iteration of the forward and real-time price-adjustment process. The two generators can trade directly with one another, and the supplier intermediates between the generators and the prosumers. Scope: Contract-network topology used in case studies A and B. The methodology does not preclude introducing direct trading relationships between generators and prosumers. Scope: Permitted extensions of the proposed bilateral P2P methodology beyond the illustrated case-study topology.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"matching","context_text":"Contract-network topology used in case studies A and B. | Forward and real-time bilateral P2P markets; agents selecting contracts with potential counterparties. | Individual contract selection during each iteration of the forward and real-time price-adjustment process. | Initialization of the price-adjustment process in each forward and real-time market; all agents. | Permitted extensions of the proposed bilateral P2P methodology beyond the illustrated case-study topology. | Potential counterparty relationships in the forward and real-time bilateral contract networks.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e16","v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E26"]},"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001"],"decision_anchor":"matching.counterparties","source_rule_sha256":"57b80f5357472bfac8b122e16f147b8129b75e8c1cd7e81fa6a785adc9dca8cc"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Agents specify potential upstream trades with sellers before initializing the buyer and seller prices used in price-signal coordination. Scope: Initialization of each forward and real-time bilateral contract market.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"p in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually dev","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\Delta p be the increment of the price-adjustment process, which is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. For market m \\in \\{F, R\\}: 1) Each agent i \\in \\mathcal{A} starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Let the full set of trades in the market be given by \\Omega_m. For agent i, the set of potential upstream trades is given by \\Omega^B_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = b(\\omega)\\} and the set of potential downstream trades is given by \\Omega^S_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = s(\\omega)\\}. 2) Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m has a buyer price p^b_\\omega and a seller price p^s_\\omega. Initially, p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its seller price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price exceeds the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price exceeds the seller price.","expression":"\\forall m \\in \\{F,R\\}, \\forall \\omega \\in \\Omega_m: \\left[\\omega \\in Y^*_{m\\,b(\\omega)} \\land \\omega \\in Z_{m\\,s(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{m\\,s(\\omega)} \\land p^b_\\omega > p^s_\\omega\\right] \\Rightarrow p^s_\\omega \\leftarrow p^s_\\omega + \\Delta p","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-pb-omega","v141-c-R002-x-ps-omega","v141-c-R002-x-Ystar-mi","v141-c-R002-x-Zstar-mi","v141-c-R002-x-Z-mi"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-delta-p"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Contract Networks for Energy Trading","anchor_text":"p y p II (ω, pω), where ω ∈ m is the underlying trade and pω is hin the price speciﬁed by the contra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each trade ω has a buyer b(ω) ∈ A and a seller s(ω) ∈ A. These are also the buyer and seller for each contract involving ω, i.e., for contract x = (ω, p_ω) we denote its buyer b(x) := b(ω) and seller s(x) := s(ω). The agents involved with a set of contracts X'_m ⊆ X_m are given by a(X'_m) := {∪_{x∈X'_m}b(x), s(x)}. Agent i has upstream contracts Y_mi := {y ∈ X_m|i = b(y)}, and downstream contracts Z_mi := {z ∈ X_m|i = s(x)}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"3) Iteratively: he a) Each agent i ∈A constructs a set of upstream p- contracts to choose from: Ymi ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"d) For each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m, the buyer and seller prices are adjusted as follows: if \\omega \\in Y^*_{mb(\\omega)} and \\omega \\in Z_{ms(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{ms(\\omega)} then if p^b_\\omega > p^s_\\omega then p^s_\\omega \\leftarrow p^s_\\omega + \\Delta p else p^b_\\omega \\leftarrow p^b_\\omega + \\Delta p end if end if"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"Price-signal coordination responds to the buyer's selection and seller's rejection of their bilateral trade by increasing its buyer price by the price-adjustment increment when the buyer price does not exceed the seller price. Scope: Individual bilateral trades during price-adjustment iterations in the forward and real-time markets. Condition: The buyer selects the trade, the seller rejects it, and the buyer price does not exceed the seller price.","expression":"\\forall m \\in \\{F,R\\}, \\forall \\omega \\in \\Omega_m: \\left[\\omega \\in Y^*_{m\\,b(\\omega)} \\land \\omega \\in Z_{m\\,s(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{m\\,s(\\omega)} \\land p^b_\\omega \\le p^s_\\omega\\right] \\Rightarrow p^b_\\omega \\leftarrow p^b_\\omega + \\Delta p","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-pb-omega","v141-c-R002-x-ps-omega","v141-c-R002-x-Ystar-mi","v141-c-R002-x-Zstar-mi","v141-c-R002-x-Z-mi"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-delta-p"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e14","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-matching","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Contract Networks for Energy Trading","anchor_text":"p y p II (ω, pω), where ω ∈ m is the underlying trade and pω is hin the price speciﬁed by the contra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Each trade ω has a buyer b(ω) ∈ A and a seller s(ω) ∈ A. These are also the buyer and seller for each contract involving ω, i.e., for contract x = (ω, p_ω) we denote its buyer b(x) := b(ω) and seller s(x) := s(ω). The agents involved with a set of contracts X'_m ⊆ X_m are given by a(X'_m) := {∪_{x∈X'_m}b(x), s(x)}. Agent i has upstream contracts Y_mi := {y ∈ X_m|i = b(y)}, and downstream contracts Z_mi := {z ∈ X_m|i = s(x)}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"3) Iteratively: he a) Each agent i ∈A constructs a set of upstream p- contracts to choose from: Ymi ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"d) For each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m, the buyer and seller prices are adjusted as follows: if \\omega \\in Y^*_{mb(\\omega)} and \\omega \\in Z_{ms(\\omega)} \\setminus Z^*_{ms(\\omega)} then if p^b_\\omega > p^s_\\omega then p^s_\\omega \\leftarrow p^s_\\omega + \\Delta p else p^b_\\omega \\leftarrow p^b_\\omega + \\Delta p end if end if"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The price-adjustment increment is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. Scope: Price-adjustment process for forward and real-time bilateral contract markets.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-delta-p"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"p in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually dev","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\Delta p be the increment of the price-adjustment process, which is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. For market m \\in \\{F, R\\}: 1) Each agent i \\in \\mathcal{A} starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Let the full set of trades in the market be given by \\Omega_m. For agent i, the set of potential upstream trades is given by \\Omega^B_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = b(\\omega)\\} and the set of potential downstream trades is given by \\Omega^S_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = s(\\omega)\\}. 2) Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m has a buyer price p^b_\\omega and a seller price p^s_\\omega. Initially, p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each trade's buyer and seller prices are initialized to zero. Scope: Each trade in the forward and real-time bilateral contract markets. Condition: At initialization of the price-adjustment process.","expression":"\\text{Initialization: } \\forall m \\in \\{F,R\\}, \\forall \\omega \\in \\Omega_m: p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-pb-omega","v141-c-R002-x-ps-omega"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"p in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually dev","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\Delta p be the increment of the price-adjustment process, which is the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts. For market m \\in \\{F, R\\}: 1) Each agent i \\in \\mathcal{A} starts by specifying the upstream trades it may be willing to make with sellers. Let the full set of trades in the market be given by \\Omega_m. For agent i, the set of potential upstream trades is given by \\Omega^B_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = b(\\omega)\\} and the set of potential downstream trades is given by \\Omega^S_{mi} = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega_{mi} | i = s(\\omega)\\}. 2) Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega_m has a buyer price p^b_\\omega and a seller price p^s_\\omega. Initially, p^b_\\omega = p^s_\\omega = 0."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The price-adjustment process is complete when an iteration produces no price changes. Scope: Price-adjustment process for each forward and real-time bilateral contract market. Condition: No price changes occur during an iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E18"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"am li- e) The price-adjustment process is complete when no est price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"e) The price-adjustment process is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. 4) The arrangement [\\tau(\\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}) | p^b] is a competitive equilibrium, where p^b is the vector of buyer prices for the trades in \\Omega_m. This coincides with a stable outcome, \\cup_{i \\in \\mathcal{A}} Y^*_{mi}."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R004-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The distributed price-adjustment process coordinates buyers' offers and sellers' acceptance or rejection to find an agreed bilateral contract network that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from. Scope: Forward and real-time bilateral P2P markets; agents selecting contracts with potential counterparties. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E19","v141-b-g02-E26"],"ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R004","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":"2031","section":"IV. Price-Adjustment Process","anchor_text":"ENERGY TRADING 2031 al- The process is scalable, since, at each iteration, only the lowest price rem","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The process is scalable, since, at each iteration, only the lowest price remaining upstream contract and highest price remaining downstream need to be considered, and the number of available contracts is reduced. ... The price-adjustment process is completed for the forward and real-time markets, so that in each market, the agents agree on a network of contracts which none of them wish to mutually deviate from. The price-adjustment process is constructed with buyers making progressively higher offers for trades to potential sellers, and sellers choosing to accept or reject the offers they receive."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-4a31f0141b-p08-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":8,"printed_page":"2033","section":"VI. Conclusion","anchor_text":"ers market uncertainty. Utility-maximising preferences for real- are time contracts and forward cont","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-08.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"A new scalable market design for P2P energy trading has been presented, using bilateral contract networks. The design includes forward and real-time markets and incorporates important characteristics particular to energy trading, including upstream-downstream energy balance and forward market uncertainty. Utility-maximising preferences for real-time contracts and forward contracts have been presented for different types of electricity market participants. It has been shown that these preferences satisfy full substitutability conditions, which ensures a distributed price-adjustment process can be used to find an agreed network of contracts that the agents do not wish to mutually deviate from."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-pb-omega","symbol":"p^b_\\omega","meaning":"Buyer price of trade \\omega in market m, adjusted during price-signal coordination.","kind":"operational","domain":"Price value for a trade; source states initialization to 0 and increments by Delta p, but does not state a broader numeric domain.","unit":null,"indices":["m","\\omega"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-pb-omega"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-ps-omega","symbol":"p^s_\\omega","meaning":"Seller price of trade \\omega in market m, adjusted during price-signal coordination.","kind":"operational","domain":"Price value for a trade; source states initialization to 0 and increments by Delta p, but does not state a broader numeric domain.","unit":null,"indices":["m","\\omega"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-ps-omega"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-Ystar-mi","symbol":"Y^*_{mi}","meaning":"Agent i's selected upstream contracts in market m; the buyer-selection side of the price-adjustment condition is \\omega in Y^*_{m b(\\omega)}.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subset of contracts X_m; source defines Y^*_{mi} = C^B_{mi}(Y_{mi}|Z_{mi}).","unit":null,"indices":["m","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-Ystar-mi"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-Zstar-mi","symbol":"Z^*_{mi}","meaning":"Agent i's selected downstream contracts in market m; seller rejection of a trade is \\omega in Z_{m s(\\omega)} minus Z^*_{m s(\\omega)}.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subset of contracts X_m; source defines Z^*_{mi} = C^S_{mi}(Z_{mi}|Y_{mi}).","unit":null,"indices":["m","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-Zstar-mi"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-Z-mi","symbol":"Z_{mi}","meaning":"Downstream contracts agent i can choose from in market m, constructed from potential downstream trades at current seller prices.","kind":"operational","domain":"Subset of contracts X_m; source constructs Z_{mi} from \\Omega^S_{mi} and current seller prices p^s_\\omega.","unit":null,"indices":["m","i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::x::v141-c-R002-x-Z-mi"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-delta-p","symbol":"\\Delta p","meaning":"Increment of the price-adjustment process; the minimum difference in price that can be specified between contracts.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E15","v141-b-g02-E17"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R002","declaration_ref":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::theta::v141-c-R002-theta-delta-p"}]},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","source_occurrences":[{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R006","market_meaning":"Iterative price-signal coordination produces mutually agreed prices for local flexibility contracts. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. Condition: At negotiation completion, when no price changes occur during an iteration. Each discrete unit of flexibility may be individually priced. Scope: Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time. Each trade is associated with at most one contract and therefore has a unique price. Scope: Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market. Condition: The selected contract set is a feasible market outcome.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. | Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time. | Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e24"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003"],"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","source_rule_sha256":"88523e59a0f17544ed3a8e30da1ef9de220d511251634127669b5e931c1b792b"},{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::V::pricing.local","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","market_meaning":"The agreed local contract price specifies the amount the seller receives from the buyer for the contracted demand reduction. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. Condition: For contracts agreed by their buyer and seller. Payments made by the DSO match the combined revenue accumulated by prosumers and aggregators in the reported outcomes. Scope: Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market. Prosumers 46-55 receive no flexibility-market revenue because they do not participate. Scope: Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. Condition: These agents do not participate in the flexibility market. Prosumers 1-30 must be compensated for their higher battery degradation costs and consequently receive significantly more revenue in Case Study B. Scope: Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. Condition: Their battery degradation costs are higher in Case Study B.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. | Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. | Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. | Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"c24209a69140811d9a59109ee0ae831784f62369582751c594a22ad2a16294e4"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Iterative price-signal coordination produces mutually agreed prices for local flexibility contracts. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. Condition: At negotiation completion, when no price changes occur during an iteration.","expression":"\\forall \\omega \\in \\Omega:\\; p^{b,(k+1)}_{\\omega}=p^{b,(k)}_{\\omega}\\;\\land\\; p^{s,(k+1)}_{\\omega}=p^{s,(k)}_{\\omega}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x001","v141-c-R006-x002"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e24","v141-b-g02-e21"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e24","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2133 The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iteration. \\cup_{i\\in A} Y^{*}_i is a stable outcome for the trading network."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ω ∈Ω. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with dow their neighbors. Each iteration: no r","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A flow chart of the price-negotiation process is shown in Fig. 2. Each agent i \\in A starts by specifying the flexibility trades they may be willing to make. Let \\Omega be the full set of trades in the market. Each trade \\omega \\in \\Omega is assigned a buyer price p^{b}_{\\omega} and a seller price p^{s}_{\\omega}. Initially, p^{b}_{\\omega} = 0 and p^{s}_{\\omega} = 0 for all \\omega \\in \\Omega. The agents then engage in iterative negotiation with their neighbors. Each iteration: Each agent i \\in A constructs a set of upstream contracts to choose from, Y_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{b}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, b(\\omega) = i\\} and a set of downstream contracts to choose from, Z_i = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p^{s}_{\\omega}) \\mid \\omega \\in \\Omega, s(\\omega) = i\\}."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each discrete unit of flexibility may be individually priced. Scope: Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E09"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Each contract specifies the same energy quantity of demand reduction, but an agent may accept multiple contracts for each time interval. The contracts discretize the prosumers’ energy flexibility (in terms of energy and time), and allow each discrete unit to be individually priced. Multi-interval trading is important, since prosumers may have storage systems with finite energy capacity. In this case, their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of demand reduction during a particular interval will depend on the price and availability of contracts at other time intervals."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Each trade is associated with at most one contract and therefore has a unique price. Scope: Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market. Condition: The selected contract set is a feasible market outcome.","expression":"\\forall \\omega \\in \\tau(X'):\\; |\\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in X'\\}| \\le 1","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R006-x003"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The agreed local contract price specifies the amount the seller receives from the buyer for the contracted demand reduction. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. Condition: For contracts agreed by their buyer and seller.","expression":"\\forall \\omega\\in\\Omega:\\ \\bigl[(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\text{ is an agreed contract with buyer }b(\\omega)\\text{ and seller }s(\\omega)\\bigr]\\Rightarrow \\text{the seller }s(\\omega)\\text{ receives }p_\\omega\\text{ from the buyer }b(\\omega).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_contract_price"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E13"],"ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"[44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their th individual net energy use (e.g. dur","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The proposed flexibility market works alongside existing retail electricity market arrangements in a distribution network. It is assumed that the distribution network has a number of prosumers–consumers with flexible energy resources, and equipment for automated resource scheduling based on user settings, such as a building or home energy management system [44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their individual net energy use (e.g. during each 30 minute interval of the day). For each interval, each prosumer is charged for their net energy use, at prices which may be time-of-use dependent, and paid for their net exported energy [45]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design; Fig. 1","anchor_text":"mer xis- ce- ies ar- Fig. 1. A trading network for distribution system ﬂexibility between a DSO, , i","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The flexibility market is made up of a network of potential flexibility contracts, with multiple contracts connecting each pair of agents that are able to trade with one another. Fig. 1 shows a high-level diagram of a trading network for distribution system flexibility. Each flexibility contract specifies a price the seller will receive from the buyer for reducing demand during a particular retail market metering interval."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"MORSTYN et al.: DESIGNING DECENTRALIZED MARKETS FOR DISTRIBUTION SYSTE large number of consumers and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Let A be the set of agents in the trading network. In the flexibility market, the agents trade flexibility contracts for a set of time intervals \\(\\mathcal{T} = \\{1, \\ldots, T\\}\\). Each flexibility contract specifies a price for a flexibility trade, specifying an agreed reduction in demand during a particular time interval. Note that all trades specify the same energy quantity of demand reduction. Let \\(X\\) be the set of contracts between the agents. A contract \\(x \\in X\\) can be described by a tuple \\((\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\), where \\(\\omega \\in \\Omega\\) is a unique trade ID, \\(t_\\omega\\) is the time interval of the underlying trade and \\(p_\\omega\\) is the price specified by the contract for the trade. The trades underlying a set of contracts \\(X\\) are given by \\(\\tau(X) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega | (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega) \\in X\\}\\)."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x001","symbol":"p^{b}_{\\omega}","meaning":"Buyer-side price of a flexibility trade ω during iterative price negotiation.","kind":"operational","domain":"price values assigned to each trade ω ∈ Ω; initialized to zero before negotiation and compared with the seller price during iterations","unit":null,"indices":["ω","iteration"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x001"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x002","symbol":"p^{s}_{\\omega}","meaning":"Seller-side price of a flexibility trade ω during iterative price negotiation.","kind":"operational","domain":"price values assigned to each trade ω ∈ Ω; initialized to zero before negotiation and compared with the buyer price during iterations","unit":null,"indices":["ω","iteration"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e21","v141-b-g02-e23"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x002"},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R006-x003","symbol":"X'","meaning":"Selected feasible outcome set of contracts whose trades determine local contract prices.","kind":"operational","domain":"feasible sets of contracts X′ ⊆ X satisfying the source feasible-outcome condition","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R006","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::x::v141-c-R006-x003"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"param_contract_price","symbol":"p_\\omega","meaning":"Price specified by an agreed flexibility contract for trade ω; the amount the seller receives from the buyer for that contract.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Contract-specific agreed price; no numeric value is specified for the general rule."},"unit":null,"indices":["ω"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E13","v141-b-g01-E22"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R007","declaration_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::theta::param_contract_price"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","stage":"L6","subtype":"bilateral_conditional_price_update","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"For each transaction, initialize p^b_ω=p^s_ω=0. When the buyer selects the transaction and the seller rejects it, if p^b_ω>p^s_ω, then p^s_ω←p^s_ω+Δp; if p^b_ω≤p^s_ω, then p^b_ω←p^b_ω+Δp. The process ends when there is no price change in a round.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: buyer price and seller price of per-transaction bilateral trades","Core relationship: when the buyer selects and the seller rejects, the corresponding side's price is increased according to the comparison of the two parties' prices","Output: updated transaction price pair"],"variation_fields":["Applies to each transaction with m∈{F,R}","The buyer selection condition is ω∈Y*_{m b(ω)}, and the seller rejection condition is ω∈Z_{m s(ω)} and ω∉Z*_{m s(ω)}","The initial buyer price and seller price are both zero","Δp is the minimum price difference that can be specified by the contract","The stopping condition is no price change in one iteration"],"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001"],"paper_id":"03-bilateral_p2p","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"coordination.price_signal","template_code":"BCT"},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","stage":"L6","subtype":"negotiated_individual_contract_price","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"A mutually agreed contract price p_ω is formed through bilateral iterative negotiation; equal-quantity, time-segmented discrete flexibility units can be priced transaction by transaction. In the feasible outcome, each selected transaction corresponds to at most one contract, so the transaction has a unique contract price.","form_kind":"procedural","identity_fields":["Object: the discrete flexibility unit corresponding to transaction ω","Formation relationship: buyer and seller agree on the contract price through iterative negotiation","Output: unique contract price p_ω for each selected transaction"],"variation_fields":["The contract corresponds to a specific retail metering period t_ω","Each contract specifies the same demand-reduction energy, and discrete units can be priced separately","Negotiation termination condition: no change in either buy-side or sell-side prices in one round","In the payment rule, the contract price represents the amount the seller receives from the buyer"],"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"],"paper_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"pricing.local","template_code":"BCT"}],"source_variants":[{"unit_id":"03-bilateral_p2p::v141-a-M01::coordination.price_signal","source_definition":"For each transaction, initialize p^b_ω=p^s_ω=0. When the buyer selects the transaction and the seller rejects it, if p^b_ω>p^s_ω, then p^s_ω←p^s_ω+Δp; if p^b_ω≤p^s_ω, then p^b_ω←p^b_ω+Δp. The process ends when there is no price change in a round.","retained_variants":["Applies to forward and real-time energy trading markets m∈{F,R}, processing per transaction ω∈Ω_m.","The trigger condition is retained as ω∈Y*_{m b(ω)} and ω∈Z_{m s(ω)}\\Z*_{m s(ω)}; prices are adjusted only when the buyer selects and the seller rejects.","If p^b_ω>p^s_ω, then p^s_ω←p^s_ω+Δp; otherwise p^b_ω←p^b_ω+Δp. Δp is the minimum price difference that can be specified by the contract.","The two-sided prices are initialized from zero; broader numerical domains and units are not given and are not added.","The direct target is retained as price-pair updating; the source describes the post-termination equilibrium arrangement using the buyer price vector, and the contract-network stability guarantee retains its perfect-substitutability condition."],"constraint_refs":["03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C001","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C002","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C003","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C004","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C005","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R002::v141-c-R002-C006","03-bilateral_p2p::v141-c-R004::v141-c-R004-C001"]},{"unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","source_definition":"A mutually agreed contract price p_ω is formed through bilateral iterative negotiation; equal-quantity, time-segmented discrete flexibility units can be priced transaction by transaction. In the feasible outcome, each selected transaction corresponds to at most one contract, so the transaction has a unique contract price.","retained_variants":["The subject matter is distribution-network demand-reduction flexibility; each contract specifies the same reduction energy and is discretized by energy and time, and each unit can be priced separately.","The contract retains the (ω,t_ω,p_ω) structure and the specific retail metering period; limited energy-storage capacity may make contract preferences in different time periods interdependent.","In each round, select the preferred upstream and downstream contract bundles and raise the prices of contracts with excess demand; the available citation does not provide the two comparison branches of 03 or the same Δp, so these details are not transplanted.","Termination requires that, for all ω∈Ω, the two-sided prices respectively satisfy p^{b,(k+1)}_ω=p^{b,(k)}_ω and p^{s,(k+1)}_ω=p^{s,(k)}_ω.","In the feasible outcome X′⊆X, each ω∈τ(X′) corresponds to at most one contract, so a selected transaction has a unique contract price; this is not extended to all potential transactions being concluded.","p_ω denotes the amount paid by the buyer to the seller for the contracted demand reduction; its contract-price role is retained and is not directly equated with the two-sided prices in the negotiation."],"constraint_refs":["22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C001","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C002","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R006::v141-c-R006-C003","22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::v141-c-R007-C001"]}],"source_parent_definition":{"definition":"A bilateral contract price formation process in which per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices serve as negotiation states, are initialized from zero, iteratively increase according to contract-selection feedback, and complete when no price changes occur in an entire round.","object":"Per-transaction bilateral contract prices with explicit buyers and sellers and their negotiation states; the subject matter of energy transactions and demand-reduction flexibility transactions is retained separately.","inputs":"Potential bilateral transactions, transaction parties, selectable contracts, contract-selection feedback, and current buy-side and sell-side prices; the feasible contract domain and price-adjustment parameters are specified according to the source.","operation":"Initialize the per-transaction buy-side and sell-side prices to zero, increase the relevant prices according to member rules based on contract-selection feedback, and repeat the negotiation until, in an entire round, both-side prices for all transactions show no change.","outputs":"Negotiation price states for per-transaction trades and the agreed prices of concluded contracts; the distinct variable roles of updated price pairs and final contract prices are retained.","boundary":"Limited to L6/BCT price formation. Contract selection serves as price feedback; contract configuration, payment execution, and resource scheduling are not included in the parent item; buyer price, seller price, and contract price are not presumed equal, and the specific increment formulas of the two members are not unified."},"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/107"});
